# Portal-side checklist — @@DEPLOYMENT_SLUG@@

Generated by `ob-builder` @@OB_BUILDER_VERSION@@ on @@BUILD_DATE@@ for
role **@@TARGET_ROLE@@**, portal `@@PORTAL_URL@@`.

Everything in this file is done **in the LemonLDAP::NG Manager**, not on the
target hosts. The artefacts next to this file cannot do it, and cannot check
that it was done: asking the portal whether these are set would need an API
that publishes the project's bastions, server groups and RPs, which is not
something an SSO should offer.

Until items 1 to 3 are in place, **any host enrolled in this project can
declare itself a bastion** on `/pam/authorize` and obtain a hop voucher (12 h
by default) for a user. That is risk **R-P1**, and for `0.7.0` it is a release
prerequisite rather than a condition of employment left to the deployment.

## 1. `pamAccessServerGroups` — required

Map each `client_id` to the `server_group` it is allowed to claim. This
artefact enrols with:

| | |
| --- | --- |
| `client_id` | `@@CLIENT_ID@@` |
| `server_group` | `@@SERVER_GROUP@@` |

Until this mapping exists, `server_group` is read **from the request body**,
which is exactly what lets a caller declare its own role.

## 2. `pamAccessAllowedRps` — required

List the RPs allowed on `/pam/*`. An **empty list means "no change"**, kept for
upgrade compatibility, so the upstream audience binding does nothing at all
until you fill it in.

This assumes the bastions are enrolled under a `client_id` of their own,
distinct from the one the rest of the project uses. If `@@CLIENT_ID@@` is
shared with your backends, split it before setting this.

## 3. Plugin version — required

The four LemonLDAP::NG plugins must be at a published version that contains
both settings. `v0.5.2` has neither.

## 4. `pamAccessBastionCertPinSourceAddress` — recommended

Pins the source address of the hop certificate. Off by default.

## 5. `allowed_bastions` on every backend — the residual defence

This one is on the hosts, not the portal:

```sh
ob-backend-setup --allowed-bastions <bastion_id>[,<id>...]
```

`ob-bastion-id` prints the id of a bastion. An empty
`/etc/open-bastion/allowed_bastions` accepts a voucher from any bastion the
SSO vouched for — which, until 1 to 3 are done, is any enrolled host.

---

References: `doc/security/09-portail-llng.md` (R-P1) and
`doc/security/08-dossier-homologation.md` (CE03, CE06, CE16, CE21).
